Conclusions
Isolated SPS in adult female patients must be thoroughly evaluated for an underlying malignancy. In
particular, a thorough physical examination must be carried out to rule out breast carcinoma. SPS which
occurs as a paraneoplastic manifestation of breast carcinoma is commonly associated with anti-
amphiphysin antibodies, which are recognized as a separate clinical entity, given their favorable response to
steroids, plasmapheresis, and removal of the underlying tumor.
